WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.383
<v Jamie>Meta just trained an AI mode that mines your Facebook history, and A M D jumped nearly seven percent on the news.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/950264/meta-ai-mode-search-facebook

00:00:07.383 --> 00:00:15.729
<v Alex>SpaceX's IPO raised ten billion more than the initial ask, which means Elon's now sitting on significant cash to pursue acquisitions without needing board permission.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgrk21wnvy9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:37.557
<v Alex>Markets rallied with Nasdaq up three point zero-seven percent, S&P up one point six-five, and the V I X collapsed eight point three-seven percent. A M D posted a six point nine-eight percent jump. Meanwhile, energy got demolished. Exxon down four point one-four percent, Chevron down three point six-four percent. It's the kind of lopsided day where the market's basically saying: AI stocks forever, fossil fuels never.

00:00:37.557 --> 00:00:43.335
<v Jamie>So the V I X dropped eight percent and nobody's even worried about what happens next week?

00:00:43.335 --> 00:01:00.027
<v Maya>No macro catalyst, no Fed announcement, no earnings surprise—just pure algorithmic muscle memory pulling capital into the places it was already going. It's like watching someone eat cereal out of the box while driving because they're too committed to the habit to stop and think about whether they're actually hungry anymore.

00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:12.867
<v Maya>Meta's letting you search across your own history through this AI Mode interface, and the AI is trained on public information to surface answers. It's like having a search engine that knows your embarrassing era better than you do.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/950264/meta-ai-mode-search-facebook

00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:19.287
<v Jamie>But Meta already has that data. Why does it matter that they're now wrapping it in an AI layer?

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:32.449
<v Alex>Because opt-in becomes a fairy tale once the AI's already trained. They're not asking permission to ingest your posts—they're announcing the product after the model's already learned from them. The permission window closed before anyone knew there was a window.

00:01:32.449 --> 00:01:49.462
<v Maya>And here's what kills me: Meta's doing this while Europe's still writing rules about AI training data, and the U.S. regulators are basically speed-dating each other trying to figure out who gets to write the memo. By the time anyone drafts a policy, Meta's already three products deep into the same playbook.

00:01:49.462 --> 00:02:02.944
<v Maya>SpaceX just closed its IPO, which means Elon's now a public company CEO for the first time, and that comes with quarterly earnings calls, investor letters, actual SEC filings where you can't just post a meme instead of answering the question.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/15/spacex-is-public-everything-you-need-to-know-post-ipo/

00:02:02.944 --> 00:02:18.673
<v Alex>The greenshoe exercise worked exactly as designed. Underwriters took the extra demand and printed more shares at the offering price, so Elon walked away with that buffer. He's now got enough dry powder to move on every acquisition he's been eyeing without asking a board for permission again.

00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:27.019
<v Jamie>So what happens in three months when SpaceX has to report earnings and the question is 'how many Starship launches actually made money this quarter?'

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:38.575
<v Alex>That's when the public markets discover that launching rockets on spec for the government doesn't have the same margin profile as selling cloud services. The 10-Q is going to be a real moment of truth.

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:49.489
<v Jamie>Trump's already floating Kevin Warsh as the next Fed Chair—and the story is less about monetary policy expertise and more about pre-positioning someone who trusts Trump before the rate-cut conversation gets politically urgent.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/15/trump-warsh-fed-reshape.html

00:02:49.489 --> 00:03:07.787
<v Alex>Warsh is a serious operator, but the timing matters. Trump's signaling this now so that when the economy softens and rate cuts become inevitable, there's already a chair in place who won't treat the Fed's independence as a reason to say no. It's not about finding the best mind—it's about finding a mind that won't argue.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:17.417
<v Maya>Trump says the Iran deal is already signed and details come pretty soon—which is geopolitical speak for 'I have a piece of paper but I'm still workshopping the ending.'
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/06/15/trump-iran-deal-questions-strait-funds

00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:33.467
<v Jamie>Fox just paid twenty-two billion dollars for Roku—which is like buying a house because you fell in love with the smart thermostat. The bet is that linear T V's future is on streaming hardware, but you're really just buying a platform to run ads on someone else's living room.

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:49.838
<v Maya>A V A R official made a hand gesture during a match and then claimed it was an involuntary, subconscious twitch—completely unaware it happened, had no intention to communicate anything. The investigation is ongoing, but the official is basically asking us to believe their hand has its own union rep.

00:03:49.838 --> 00:04:02.999
<v Alex>So the defense is that their body committed a gesture without consulting their brain first. That's the line. And somewhere in a disciplinary hearing, a lawyer is actually arguing that muscle memory can't be held accountable for its own expressions.

00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:15.518
<v Jamie>Every ref who's ever made a terrible call just found their legal precedent. 'Your Honor, I threw the flag involuntarily. My arm and I have a complicated relationship.' This person's handed every whistleblower in sports a permission structure.

00:04:15.518 --> 00:04:22.580
<v Maya>Meta's betting that mining your past self for content recommendations feels less creepy once you're the one searching instead of them.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:27.074
<v Alex>Rebranding surveillance as self-service doesn't change what's being watched—just who holds the remote.

00:04:27.074 --> 00:04:31.568
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
